[" Here's a C++ solution using a ring buffer with a custom allocator to manage memory, a mutex for synchronization, and a circular queue implementation for efficient handling of log messages. The code uses the `std::atomic` library for atomic operations and includes a mechanism to prevent the buffer from exceeding its size limit.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <cstring>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>

constexpr size_t kMaxSize = 10'000;
constexpr size_t kBufferSize = 1'024' * sizeof(char); // 1 MB

class CircularBuffer {
public:
    void Append(const char* message, size_t len) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(_mutex);
        while (_queue.size() >= kMaxSize) {
            _condition.wait(lock);
        }

        // Allocate memory with custom allocator to minimize contention
        auto data = _allocator.Allocate();
        std::memcpy(data, message, len);
        auto end = data + len;
        _allocator.Construct(data + len, 0); // Default-construct new elements at the end of the buffer

        // Update queue and adjust size if necessary
        _queue.push_back({data, end});
        size_t currentSize = std::distance(_queue.cbegin(), _queue.cend()) * kBufferSize;
        if (currentSize > kBufferSize) {
            size_t elementsToRemove = (currentSize - kBufferSize + kBufferSize % sizeof(char)) / kBufferSize;
            auto it = _queue.cbegin();
            std::advance(it, elementsToRemove);
            _allocator.Deallocate(*it); // Deallocate the oldest entries
            _queue.erase(_queue.cbegin(), it);
        }

        _condition.notify_one();
    }

private:
    struct Element {
        char* begin;
        char* end;
    };

    std::mutex _mutex;
    std::condition_variable _condition;
    std::queue<Element> _queue;
    std::vector<char> _buffer; // Holds the actual data (circular buffer)
    std::allocator<char> _allocator{_buffer.data(), _buffer.data() + kBufferSize}; // Custom allocator for minimizing contention
};
```

When using a mutex for synchronization, the trade-offs are better ease of implementation and lower complexity compared to implementing a lock-free approach. However, using a mutex may lead to increased contention and potentially higher latency in concurrent writes due to locks being acquired and released. Implementing a lock-free data structure would reduce this contention but come at the cost of greater complexity and potential performance overhead in managing the concurrency control mechanisms themselves. The choice between these approaches depends on your specific application requirements, such as the number of threads accessing the buffer concurrently, the frequency of writes, and the available hardware resources."]

