['```javascript
class DataBuffer {
  constructor(windowSize) {
    this.windowSize = windowSize;
    this.buffer = new Map();
    this.activeStreams = new Set();
  }

  addStream(streamId, iterator) {
    if (!this.activeStreams.has(streamId)) {
      this.activeStreams.add(streamId);
      let current = this.buffer.get(streamId) || [];
      const observer = {
        next: (value) => {
          let keyExist = this.buffer.has(streamId);
          if (!keyExist) this.buffer.set(streamId, []);
          const currentValues = this.buffer.get(streamId);
          let isDup = false;
          for (let i = Math.max(0, currentValues.length - this.windowSize); i < currentValues.length; ++i) {
            if (currentValues[i].equals(value)) {
              isDup = true;
              break;
            }
          }
          if (!isDup) currentValues.push(value);
          if (currentValues.length > this.windowSize) currentValues.shift();
        },
        error: (error) => console.error(`Error in stream ${streamId}: ${error}`),
        complete: () => { /* No action */ }
      };
      streamId.forEachValue(iterator, observer);
    }
  }

  removeStream(streamId) {
    if (this.activeStreams.has(streamId)) {
      this.activeStreams.delete(streamId);
    }
  }

  getData() {
    const result = [];
    for (let value of this.buffer.values()) {
      result.push(...value);
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

This code defines a `DataBuffer` class, which serves as a unified buffer for multiple data streams. The `addStream()` method is used to add new streams and handle their data, while the `removeStream()` function takes care of removing streams when no longer needed. The `getData()` method returns all the accumulated data.

The class uses a Map to store the data from each stream and an Set to keep track of active streams. A sliding window mechanism is implemented using the windowSize parameter, ensuring that only a specified amount of data is kept in memory at any given time. The `equals()` function should be defined based on your specific data type requirements for checking duplicates.

This code assumes you have an iterator-based asynchronous data stream API (such as async iterators or Generators) and the Stream module from Node.js (or similar libraries for other environments).']

[" Here is a JavaScript function named `traverseDOM` that meets your requirements:

```javascript
class Traversal {
  constructor(callback, queue = [document.body]) {
    this.callback = callback;
    this.queue = queue;
  }

  async start() {
    try {
      while (this.queue.length) {
        const node = this.queue.shift();

        if (!node || !node.parentNode) continue; // Prevent infinite recursion

        const isAsync = typeof node.getBoundingClientRect === 'function';
        let result = null;

        if (isAsync) {
          await setTimeout(() => {
            result = this.traverseNode(node);
          }, 0);
        } else {
          result = this.traverseNode(node);
        }

        if (result) this.callback(node, result);
        const newQueueNodes = [...this.collectNodesToTraverse(node)];
        this.queue.push(...newQueueNodes);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error during traversal:', error);
    }
  }

  traverseNode(node) {
    const visitNode = () => {
      if (!node || !node.parentNode) return false;

      const attrs = ['id', 'class']; // Add more attributes as needed
      let match = false;

      for (const attr of attrs) {
        if (node[attr] === this._attrValue) {
          match = true;
          break;
        }
      }

      const isAsyncNode = typeof node.getBoundingClientRect === 'function';

      if (match || (!isAsyncNode && visitNode.next)) {
        return true;
      }

      this._attrValue = undefined; // Reset the attribute value for the next call
      if (isAsyncNode) return false; // Async nodes are handled in the loop

      const children = [...node.children];
      this._attrValue = node[attrs[0]] || node[attrs[1]]; // Get the first matching attribute value
      return children.some(visitNode);
    };

    if (typeof visitNode === 'function') {
      return visitNode();
    }
  }

  collectNodesToTraverse(node) {
    const children = [...node.children];
    const queue = [];

    for (const child of children) {
      if (!child || !child.parentNode) continue;

      if (child !== node && !(child.hasAttribute('data-ignore'))) {
        queue.push(child);
      }
    }

    return queue;
  }
}

// Usage:
const traverse = new Traversal((node, attrs) => {
  // Do something with the node and its attributes
});
traverse.start();
```

This implementation uses a queue-based approach to traverse the DOM tree efficiently. It handles both synchronous and asynchronous components using `setTimeout`. The `Traversal` class also ensures that it doesn't get stuck in infinite recursion due to circular references by checking if nodes have parents and whether they are already in the queue.

The `traverseDOM` function takes a callback function for processing elements with specific attributes, which allows it to accommodate future changes in the DOM structure or additional attributes for testing. The solution is flexible enough to integrate into an existing web application, taking into account potential integration issues and dependencies on external libraries or services."]

