[" Here's a simplified version of the requested C# function using `System.Security.Cryptography` for hash generation and `System.IO` for file handling. This code uses a rolling hash approach, where only a fixed-size chunk of the log line is hashed at a time to manage memory usage efficiently.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;
using System.IO;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace LoggingFramework
{
    public class RollingHashLogger : ILogger
    {
        private const int HashChunkSize = 1024;
        private readonly SHA256 _hashFunction = SHA256.Create();
        private byte[] _hashValue;

        public Task LogAsync(string message)
        {
            if (_hashValue == null || _hashValue.Length < _hashValue.LongLength)
                _hashValue = new byte[HashFunction.HashSize / 8];

            var chunk = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(message).Take(HashChunkSize).ToArray();

            using (var hmac = _hashFunction.CreateHMAC())
            {
                hmac.TransformBlock(chunk, 0, chunk.Length, _hashValue, 0);
            }

            return Task.CompletedTask;
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation creates a rolling hash logger that inherits from the `ILogger` interface. The `LogAsync` method takes in a message (log line), generates a rolling hash using SHA256, and manages memory usage by hashing only a fixed-size chunk of the log line at a time.

To integrate this into an existing logging framework supporting concurrent processing and variable throughput, you can modify your logging infrastructure to accept an `ILogger` instance as a parameter in its constructor or configuration settings. Replace the default logger with the new rolling hash logger where appropriate within your logging system.

For future scalability, consider creating a more flexible abstraction for handling additional data fields without significant rework. You can do this by creating an interface `IHashedData` that defines a single method `GetHashableData()`, which returns the part of the object to be hashed. The rolling hash logger should be updated to accept an instance of `IHashedData` instead of a string message. This allows for easy integration with other data types and future expansions.

Regarding memory consumption, this implementation generates a fixed-size byte array for the hash value and updates it with each log line. While this approach ensures efficient memory usage, make sure that your logging infrastructure properly handles garbage collection to minimize potential performance impacts. You can use profiling tools or monitor application memory consumption to determine if further optimizations are necessary."]

