The pthread_mutex_lock() and pthread_mutex_unlock() functions are used to ensure thread-safety when accessing the queue concurrently from multiple threads. The MessageQueue structure includes a lock that should be acquired before modifying the queue contents.

To handle high volumes of messages efficiently, consider using an appropriate data structure based on your specific use case. For example, if you expect frequent insertions at the tail end of the queue and infrequent deletions, an array-based approach might be more suitable. On the other hand, if both insertion and deletion operations are expected to occur frequently throughout the queue, a linked list or skip list could offer better performance.

To enable horizontal scaling, you may want to implement multiple MessageQueue instances for different user groups or priority levels and distribute messages among them accordingly. This can be achieved by implementing load balancing techniques such as round-robin scheduling or hash-based distribution.

