The code handles potential allocation failures gracefully by dropping new data when the array is full and returning an empty structure when popping from an empty array. The fallback strategy used here is simple but effective, as it allows the function to continue operating with reduced functionality under heavy load conditions.

To extend the function, a `switch` statement is provided at the end of the code that allows for future tags to be added without altering the core logic of the dynamic array management. The existing codebase remains unchanged by this addition.

The design decision to use a mutex ensures thread-safety, but it may introduce some latency during peak traffic periods due to lock contention. To minimize this impact, consider using more advanced synchronization mechanisms such as locks with lower overhead or optimistic concurrency control in cases where you have fine-grained access to the data.

