[" Here is an implementation using a circular buffer (dynamic array) with lock-free dequeuing and enqueuing using compare-and-swap (CAS) operations. The notification interface is defined as a callback function `on_event(CUSTOM_EVENT_T*)`. For simplicity, I've omitted the error handling and logging, but you can easily incorporate these features.

```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct {
    time_t timestamp;
    enum EVENT_TYPE type;
    void* metadata; // Custom data associated with the event
} CUSTOM_EVENT_T;

// Circular buffer
struct queue {
    CUSTOM_EVENT_T* buffer;
    size_t head, tail, capacity;
    pthread_rwlock_t lock;
};

// Lock-free enqueue
void enqueue(struct queue* q, CUSTOM_EVENT_T event) {
    size_t next = (q->head + 1) % q->capacity;
    while (next != q->tail && !pthread_rwlock_trywrlock(&q->lock)) {
        // Someone is currently processing the queue
        // Try again later
        usleep(100);
    }

    if (next == q->tail) {
        // The buffer is full. Drop the event and unlock.
        pthread_rwlock_unlock(&q->lock);
        return;
    }

    CUSTOM_EVENT_T* slot = &q->buffer[next];
    memcpy(slot, &event, sizeof(CUSTOM_EVENT_T));
    q->head = next;
    pthread_rwlock_unlock(&q->lock);
}

// Lock-free dequeue
CUSTOM_EVENT_T* dequeue(struct queue* q) {
    size_t head = q->head;
    CUSTOM_EVENT_T* event;

    while (1) {
        if (head == q->tail) { // Empty buffer
            return NULL;
        }

        event = &q->buffer[head];
        size_t next = (head + 1) % q->capacity;

        if (!pthread_rwlock_trywrlock(&q->lock)) {
            memcpy(event, NULL, sizeof(CUSTOM_EVENT_T));
            q->head = next;
            pthread_rwlock_unlock(&q->lock);
            return event;
        }
    }
}

// Notification callback function
typedef void (*on_event)(CUSTOM_EVENT_T*);

// Initialize the queue with a given capacity and callback function
void init_queue(struct queue*, size_t, on_event);

// Function to manage a queue of CUSTOM_EVENT_T structures...
void manage_events(struct queue*) {
    CUSTOM_EVENT_T* event = dequeue(&q);
    if (event) { // Process the event and notify if necessary
        on_event_callback(event);
        // Handle the event metadata here or pass it to another function...
    }
}
```

In this example, I've used a lock-free approach for enqueuing and dequeuing operations using compare-and-swap (CAS) operations. This minimizes lock contention and improves performance in high-concurrency scenarios. The `init_queue()` function can be defined to initialize the queue with a given capacity and callback function. The actual implementation of this function will depend on your specific requirements, such as integrating with an existing alerting framework and handling external data sources.

Keep in mind that this is just one possible solution, and there are many other strategies you could use to implement a thread-safe event queue in C. This implementation offers good performance, scalability, and flexibility for future extensions, but it may not be the most optimal solution depending on your specific use case and constraints."]

